{
  // Output filename and BoardLoader data-variable name:
  //   boardName "RP2040" -> designer/boards/RP2040/RP2040.json -> const RP2040Data = {...}
  "boardName": "RP2040",

  // Fallback human-readable name shown in the designer UI.  Overridden
  // by the matching `<id>.name=` entry in a sibling-or-ancestor
  // boards.txt when one is present (matched via `<id>.build.variant=`
  // against this variant's directory name) — for this board that's
  // "ElectronicCats HunterCat NFC RP2040" (variants/rp2040/boards.txt).
  "displayName": "Raspberry Pi Pico (RP2040)",

  // Same parser dialect as every other RP2040 board — see
  // ../board.json for the full rationale.
  "family": "rp2040",

  "inputOnlyGpios": [],

  // Default transports — Serial only. This board's onboard NFC hardware
  // (NXP PN7150) is reached over the default I2C bus (Wire0, GPIO4/GPIO5
  // already declared in this board's own pins_arduino.h), not a network
  // transport, so it does not affect this bucketing. No onboard WiFi/BLE
  // radio exists on this board, so plain "rp2040" (not "rp2040w") is
  // correct.
  "connections": ["serial"],

  "pinNotes": {},

  // pfodWeb NOTE (dedicated-hardware pin-exclusion + LED/button tagging
  // audit, 2026-07-13):
  //
  // Board: Electronic Cats "Hunter Cat NFC" (RP2040 hardware revision,
  // "HunterCat NFC RP2040" per its own boards.txt display name / USB
  // product string — a later RP2040-based redesign of the original
  // SAMD21-based Hunter Cat NFC; both share the product name but this
  // variant is exclusively the RP2040 build). Real, well-documented
  // open-hardware NFC security-research/pentesting tool combining an
  // RP2040 with an onboard NXP PN7150 NFC controller.
  //
  // Sources consulted:
  //   - https://github.com/ElectronicCats/HunterCatNFC (official open-hardware
  //     repo; CERN-OHL-1.2 hardware, AGPL-3.0 firmware) — repo README and
  //     releases confirm the "v2.041"/"v2.040" tags are the RP2040 hardware
  //     revision ("New Chip RP2040!!"), superseding v1.x SAMD21 boards.
  //   - This board's own official Arduino firmware, all 3 example sketches
  //     under Firmware/ (hunterCatNFC_AllOne, huntercat_nfc_detect_readers,
  //     HunterCatNFC_P2P), each independently containing the identical
  //     `#ifdef ARDUINO_ARCH_RP2040` block:
  //       #define PIN_LED  8
  //       #define PIN_LED2 9
  //       #define PIN_LED3 10
  //       #define BUTTON_0 19
  //       #define BUTTON_1 20
  //       #define BUTTON_2 21
  //     and (for the non-SAMD/RP2040 `#else` branch shared with the PN7150
  //     pin macros):
  //       #define PN7150_IRQ (18)
  //       #define PN7150_VEN (17)
  //     `PIN_LED`/GPIO8 matches this board's own upstream arduino-pico
  //     `pins_arduino.h` (`#define PIN_LED (8u)`), confirming LED_BUILTIN
  //     is GPIO8 — cross-validated between the Arduino core variant file
  //     and three independent official firmware sketches.
  //   - The same firmware's `RGB(int R,int G,int B)` helper
  //     (`analogWrite(PIN_LED,R); analogWrite(PIN_LED2,B); analogWrite(PIN_LED3,g);`)
  //     confirms all three LED pins are PWM-driven (brightness/colour
  //     mixed), and `blink()` writes HIGH to turn a LED on (active-high).
  //     `pinMode(BUTTON_n, INPUT_PULLUP)` + `digitalRead(BUTTON_n)==0` in
  //     hunterCatNFC_AllOne.ino confirms the 3 buttons are active-low
  //     (pressed = LOW), matching the product's "3 LEDs status"/multi-mode
  //     button description from electroniccats.com marketing copy.
  //   - `Electroniccats_PN7150` library (ElectronicCats/ElectronicCats-PN7150,
  //     src/Electroniccats_PN7150.h/.cpp): constructor default parameter
  //     `TwoWire *wire = &Wire` and `_wire->begin()` in `connectNCI()` (no
  //     `setSDA()`/`setSCL()` call — that's commented-out example code, not
  //     executed) confirms the PN7150 talks over the DEFAULT Wire bus, i.e.
  //     this board's own already-declared `PIN_WIRE0_SDA (4u)` /
  //     `PIN_WIRE0_SCL (5u)` — not a separate/second I2C bus. No evidence
  //     of a Stemma QT/Qwiic external I2C header on this specific product
  //     was found, and `WIRE_HOWMANY (1u)` in this board's own
  //     pins_arduino.h confirms only one I2C bus exists at all, so GPIO4/5
  //     are treated as dedicated to the onboard PN7150.
  //   - A separate manual trace of this board's own KiCad schematic
  //     (Hardware/HunterCat-NFC/HunterCat-NFC.kicad_sch at git tag v2.041)
  //     was attempted first and produced a DIFFERENT (GPIO8/9=IRQ/VEN,
  //     GPIO16/17/18=LEDs, GPIO21/22=SCL/SDA, GPIO5/6/7=buttons) mapping.
  //     That schematic-geometry trace is NOT used here — it disagreed with
  //     three mutually-consistent, independent official firmware sketches
  //     and with upstream arduino-pico's own PIN_LED=8 definition, and a
  //     manual KiCad pin-coordinate trace without a rendered view is more
  //     error-prone than reading the vendor's own shipped, working firmware
  //     source. The firmware+arduino-pico-core agreement is treated as
  //     authoritative per this audit's "confirm via real vendor
  //     documentation" standard.
  //
  // Full findings/sources trail:
  // boardsDetails/rp2040/rp2040/electroniccats_huntercat_nfc/notes.txt
  //
  // Per-pin reasoning:
  //   GPIO4  - PIN_WIRE0_SDA - onboard PN7150 NFC controller's I2C data
  //            line (default Wire bus; this board has only one I2C bus,
  //            WIRE_HOWMANY=1u, used exclusively by the PN7150 per its own
  //            official firmware). capabilities left empty; i2c_sda is
  //            auto-supplied by build_boards.js from this board's Wire0
  //            SDA alias, so digital_input/digital_output/pwm_output are
  //            stripped while the correct single bus-role capability
  //            remains (same pattern as challenger_2040_nfc's GPIO10/11).
  //   GPIO5  - PIN_WIRE0_SCL - onboard PN7150 NFC controller's I2C clock
  //            line. Same reasoning as GPIO4 (i2c_scl auto-supplied).
  //   GPIO8  - PIN_LED / LED_BUILTIN - onboard status LED, red channel.
  //            Confirmed active-high (firmware's blink() writes HIGH to
  //            turn on) and PWM-dimmed (analogWrite(PIN_LED,R) in the
  //            firmware's RGB() helper). Tagged led_high + pwm_output;
  //            digital_input dropped.
  //   GPIO9  - PIN_LED2 - onboard status LED, blue channel (same RGB status
  //            indicator as GPIO8/GPIO10, per firmware's RGB() helper:
  //            analogWrite(PIN_LED2,B)). Same tagging as GPIO8.
  //   GPIO10 - PIN_LED3 - onboard status LED, green channel (per firmware's
  //            RGB() helper: analogWrite(PIN_LED3,g)). Same tagging as
  //            GPIO8/GPIO9. (Tagged as 3 independent PWM-driven led_high
  //            pins rather than led_neopixel — this is a discrete 3-channel
  //            RGB LED driven directly by 3 RP2040 GPIOs/PWM slices, not a
  //            single-wire WS2812-style addressable pixel.)
  //   GPIO17 - PN7150_VEN - onboard PN7150 NFC controller's reset/enable
  //            line, driven by the RP2040 (HIGH-LOW-HIGH reset pulse in
  //            the PN7150 library's connectNCI()). Not general-purpose. No
  //            bus alias on this pin, so capabilities left empty outright.
  //   GPIO18 - PN7150_IRQ - onboard PN7150 NFC controller's interrupt
  //            output, read by the RP2040. Not general-purpose. No bus
  //            alias on this pin, so capabilities left empty outright.
  //   GPIO19 - BUTTON_0 - user mode-select pushbutton, confirmed active-low
  //            (INPUT_PULLUP + digitalRead()==0 in official firmware).
  //            Tagged digital_input + button.
  //   GPIO20 - BUTTON_1 - user mode-select pushbutton. Same as GPIO19.
  //   GPIO21 - BUTTON_2 - user mode-select pushbutton. Same as GPIO19.
  //
  // GPIO8/9/10/17/18/19/20/21 carry no SPI/I2C/other bus alias on this
  // board's own pins_arduino.h, so the build_boards.js unconditional
  // bus-capability auto-append (the "confirmed button/LED pin also aliased
  // as SDA/SCL/SS/MOSI/MISO/SCK" bug class flagged for this audit) does not
  // apply to them. GPIO4/5 DO carry the Wire0 SDA/SCL alias, but that alias
  // is the correct, intentional dedicated-hardware role itself (not an
  // accidental collision with a spare bus) — no pins_arduino.h edit was
  // needed.
  //
  // No separate onboard NeoPixel found (this board uses a discrete 3-pin
  // PWM RGB LED, not a WS2812-style addressable pixel — see GPIO8/9/10
  // reasoning above). No onboard user button beyond BUTTON_0/1/2 (GPIO19/
  // 20/21) and the standard reset/BOOTSEL (not a normal GPIO, out of scope
  // per the same reasoning already documented in rpipico/board.json).
  "chipGpios": {
    "4": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_WIRE0_SDA - onboard NXP PN7150 NFC controller's I2C data line (default/only Wire bus on this board). i2c_sda capability is auto-supplied by build_boards.js from this board's Wire0 SDA alias." },
    "5": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_WIRE0_SCL - onboard NXP PN7150 NFC controller's I2C clock line. i2c_scl capability is auto-supplied by build_boards.js from this board's Wire0 SCL alias." },
    "8": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "led_high", "pwm_output"], "note": "PIN_LED / LED_BUILTIN - onboard status LED, red channel of a 3-pin PWM RGB LED. Confirmed active-high and PWM-dimmed via this board's own official firmware (blink()/RGB() helpers, all 3 example sketches)." },
    "9": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "led_high", "pwm_output"], "note": "PIN_LED2 - onboard status LED, blue channel of the same 3-pin PWM RGB LED as GPIO8/GPIO10. Confirmed via this board's own official firmware RGB() helper." },
    "10": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "led_high", "pwm_output"], "note": "PIN_LED3 - onboard status LED, green channel of the same 3-pin PWM RGB LED as GPIO8/GPIO9. Confirmed via this board's own official firmware RGB() helper." },
    "17": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PN7150_VEN - onboard NXP PN7150 NFC controller's reset/enable line, driven by the RP2040. Not general-purpose." },
    "18": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PN7150_IRQ - onboard NXP PN7150 NFC controller's interrupt output, read by the RP2040. Not general-purpose." },
    "19": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "BUTTON_0 - onboard user mode-select pushbutton. Confirmed active-low (INPUT_PULLUP + digitalRead()==0) via this board's own official firmware." },
    "20": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "BUTTON_1 - onboard user mode-select pushbutton. Same as GPIO19/BUTTON_0." },
    "21": { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "button"], "note": "BUTTON_2 - onboard user mode-select pushbutton. Same as GPIO19/BUTTON_0." }
  },

  // Same 8-bit analogWrite() default as every other RP2040/RP2350 board.
  "pwm": { "min": 0, "max": 255, "method": "analogWrite" },

  // No hardware DAC on RP2040/RP2350.
  "dac": {},

  // RP2040's one 12-bit ADC (0-4095), ~3.3V reference — same as every
  // other RP2040 board. Matches this board's own pins_arduino.h
  // (NUM_ANALOG_INPUTS=3, ADC_RESOLUTION=12u).
  "adc": { "bits": 12, "max": 4095, "defaultRefVolts": "3.3" }
}
